To create a text editor that includes multiple lists (like bulleted or numbered lists) using Angular, you can follow these steps:

1. **Create the Text Editor Component**: This component will handle the main functionality of editing and rendering the text.
2. **Handle List Types**: The list types (bulleted or numbered) should be managed within this component.
3. **Use HTML5 Input Elements**: For better browser support and accessibility, use HTML5 input elements like `<input type="text">` for plain text and `<textarea>` for rich text.

Here's an example implementation:

### Step 1: Create the Text Editor Component

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-text-editor',
  templateUrl: './text-editor.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./text-editor.component.css']
})
export class TextEditorComponent {
  public text = '';
  private currentListType = 'bullet';
  private listItems = [];

  constructor() {}

  onInputChange(event: any): void {
    const value = event.target.value;
    if (!this.listItems.includes(value)) {
      this.listItems.push(this.currentListType === 'bullet' ? '-' : '1.');
    }
    this.text = value;
  }

  toggleListType(): void {
    this.currentListType = this.currentListType === 'bullet' ? 'numbered' : 'bullet';
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Update `index.html`

Add the necessary HTML elements and configure them properly:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Text Editor</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.0.0-beta3/css/all.min.css">
  <style>
    .list-item {
      margin-left: 20px;
      display: inline-block;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="root"></div>

<script src="path/to/app.js"></script>
<script>
  document.getElementById('root').innerHTML = `
    <app-text-editor [(ngModel)]="editorContent"></app-text-editor>
  `;
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Step 3: Implement the Renderer Logic

In your Angular app's main file (`app.module.ts`), make sure you have imported the necessary modules and providers.

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms'; // Import FormsModule for form validation
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { TextEditorComponent } from './text-editor/text-editor.component';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent,
    TextEditorComponent
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule,
    FormsModule
  ],
  providers: [],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
```

### Final Note:
This example uses a simple approach with plain text input and toggling between bullet points and numbers. In a real-world application, consider adding more features such as undo/redo capabilities, spell-checking, syntax highlighting, etc., depending on the specific requirements of your project.

Remember to adjust the CSS styles according to your design preferences. Also, ensure that the script tag for the text editor component is correctly placed so it loads before other scripts in your page.

